---

## 1. Core Concept: Intercepting the Back Button

When a modal opens, push a new entry to the browser history. On a back button press (i.e., a `popstate` event), check whether a modal is open. If so, close the modal and prevent the default navigation.

---

## 2. Implementation Example

### Step 1: Define Modal Helpers

```javascript
class ModalManager {
  constructor() {
    this.isOpen = false;
    this.modalState = null;
    this.setupHistoryListener();
  }

  setupHistoryListener() {
    window.addEventListener('popstate', this.handlePopState.bind(this));
  }

  handlePopState(event) {
    if (this.isOpen && event.state?.modal) {
      this.isOpen = false;
      this.closeModal();
      event.preventDefault(); // Prevent default navigation
    }
  }

  openModal(modalId, data = {}) {
    this.isOpen = true;
    this.modalState = { modal: true, modalId, data };

    // Push a new history entry
    window.history.pushState(this.modalState, '', window.location.href);

    // Show UI
    document.getElementById(modalId).style.display = 'block';
  }

  closeModal() {
    this.isOpen = false;
    document.querySelectorAll('.modal').forEach(modal => {
      modal.style.display = 'none';
    });

    // Remove the history entry that corresponds to the modal
    window.history.replaceState(null, '', window.location.href);
  }
}

const modalManager = new ModalManager();
```

