# Handling Back Button in Single-Page Apps with Modals

To prevent the browser's back button from navigating away from the page when a modal is open, you can use the **History API** and the **`popstate` event**. This allows the back button to close the modal instead of leaving the page.

## Solution Overview

1. **Push a new history state** when the modal opens.
2. **Intercept the `popstate` event** to detect back button clicks.
3. **Close the modal** if it's open; otherwise, proceed with normal navigation.

## Implementation Example (Vanilla JavaScript)

```javascript
class ModalManager {
  constructor() {
    this.isOpen = false;
    this.modalStack = []; // Track nested modals
    this.init();
  }

  init() {
    if (this.isOpen) {
      window.history.pushState(null, '', window.location.href);
    }

    window.addEventListener('popstate', (event) => {
      if (this.isOpen) {
        this.closeModal();
      }
    });
  }

  openModal(modalId) {
    this.isOpen = true;
    this.modalStack.push(modalId);
    window.history.pushState(null, '', window.location.href + '?modal=' + modalId);
    document.getElementById(modalId).classList.add('active');
  }

  closeModal() {
    if (this.modalStack.length > 0) {
      this.modalStack.pop();
      this.isOpen = this.modalStack.length > 0;
      window.history.back();
      document.querySelectorAll('.modal.active').forEach(modal => {
        modal.classList.remove('active');
      });
    } else {
      this.isOpen = false;
      window.history.back();
    }
  }
}

// Usage
const modalManager = new ModalManager();

document.getElementById('openModalBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  modalManager.openModal('myModal');
});

document.getElementById('closeModalBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  modalManager.closeModal();
});
```

