### 2. React Implementation (Hooks)

In React, you want to ensure this logic is cleaned up when the component unmounts to prevent memory leaks or unexpected behavior.

```jsx
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';

const Modal = ({ isOpen, onClose }) => {
  useEffect(() => {
    // When the modal opens, push a state
    if (isOpen) {
      window.history.pushState({ modalOpen: true }, "");
    }

    // Handle the back button
    const handlePopState = () => {
      if (isOpen) {
        onClose(); // Trigger your state update to close modal
      }
    };

    window.addEventListener('popstate', handlePopState);

    // Cleanup: Remove listener and handle history when component unmounts
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('popstate', handlePopState);
    };
  }, [isOpen, onClose]);

  if (!isOpen) return null;

  return (
    <div className="modal">
      <button onClick={onClose}>Close</button>
      <p>Modal Content</p>
    </div>
  );
};
```

---

### ⚠️ The "Manual Close" Edge Case (Important!)

There is one major UX issue with the `pushState` method: **What happens if the user closes the modal by clicking an "X" button instead of the back button?**

If they click the "X", the history stack still contains that "dummy" state you pushed. If they then click the browser's back button, it will just close the modal again (or do nothing) instead of taking them to the previous page.

**The Fix:**
When the user clicks your manual "X" or "Close" button, you must manually trigger a `history.back()` call to "pop" that dummy state off the stack.

**Updated Logic:**
```javascript
